
Probability
======================================

Bernoulli trials
------------------

A fair coin is tossed 7 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 3 heads.

The probability of having the first 3 tosses heads and the rest tails is :math:`\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^7`. This is one ordering of heads and tails. How many orderings are possible? We need to select 3 out of 7 positions for heads, the rest will be tails. This can be done in :math:`C(7,3)` ways. Thus, the probability of getting exactly 3 heads and 4 tails in any order is

.. math::

   C(7,3)\times \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^7

Conditional probability
-------------------------

Consider the following Venn diagram:

.. image:: tripleAintB.png
  :width: 225

The set :math:`u` contains all the animals in a jungle, the set :math:`A` contains all the carnivorous animals in the jungle, the set :math:`B` contains all the mammals in the jungle, and set :math:`C` contains all the animals in the jungle that can climb trees.

The probability that a random animal in the jungle is a carnivore is

.. math::

   P(A) = \frac{\#A}{\#u}.

However, if we have the information that this random animal is a mammal, the the probability that this animal is a carnivore is

.. math::

   P(A|B) = \frac{\#A\cap B}{\#B}.

The notation :math:`P(A|B)` is for probability of A given B is true, or it is the probability of A conditional on B.

We can simplify this as

.. math::

   P(A|B) = \frac{\#A\cap B}{\#B} = \frac{\frac{\#A\cap B}{\#u}}{\frac{\#B}{\#u}}=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}.

Thus,

.. math::

   P(A|B) = \frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}~\text{or}~P(A\cap B)=P(A|B)\cdot P(B).

======================================

Consider another situation. The probability of a fair coin resulting in heads when tossed is :math:`\frac{1}{2}`. If we toss the coin and the result is heads, what is the probability that it will result in heads when it is tossed a second time. Clearly, the result of the first toss does not change the probability of getting heads in the second toss. In this case,

.. math::

   P(\text{Heads on the second toss}|\text{Heads on the first toss})=P(\text{Heads on the second toss})

Thus,

.. math::

   & P(\text{Heads on the second toss}~\cap~\text{Heads on the first toss})

   &= P(\text{Heads on the second toss}|\text{Heads on the first toss})\cdot P(\text{Heads on the first toss})

   &= P(\text{Heads on the second toss})\cdot P(\text{Heads on the first toss})

Such, events are called "independent" events. The conditional probability is just the unconditional probability. **The probability of the intersection of two independent events is the product of the probability of each event**.

Some more examples:

Whether it will rain today and whether it is a Monday today are independent events.

When two dice are rolled, the probability of getting 1 on the first die and 4 on the second die are independent events.


Mutually exclusive events
---------------------------

The probability of a person getting up before 7 in the morning on a weekday is 0.9. The probability of the person getting up before 7 in the morning on a weekend is 0.2. What is the probability of the person getting up before 7 on a random day?

There are two possibilites that cover all days - this random day is either a weekday or a weekend day. Moreover the day cannot both be a weekday and a weekend day. **Such possibilities that are disjoint (have no overlap/intersection) are called "mutually exclusive" events. When these events cover all possibilities, they are called "mutually exclusive exhaustive" events**. They are useful in the following way:

.. math::

   & P(\text{person will get up before 7 today})

   & = P(\text{person will get up before 7}~\cap~\text{today is a weekday}) + P(\text{person will get up before 7}~\cap~\text{today is a weekend})

   & = P(\text{person will get up before 7}|\text{today is a weekday})\cdot P(\text{today is a weekday})

   &+ P(\text{person will get up before 7}|\text{today is a weekend})\cdot P(\text{today is a weekend})

   &= 0.9\times \frac{5}{7} + 0.2\times \frac{2}{7} = 0.7

======================================

Another example is if we are trying to compute the probability whether it will snow on a random day, the relevant mutually exclusive exhaustive events would be the month of the year in which the random day could fall.

The day of the week still forms a set of mutually exclusive exhaustive events. But it is not very relevant to finding whether it would snow on that day.

